==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|finishen}}, {{m|enm|finisshen}}, {{m|enm|finischen}}, from {{uder|en|fro||finiss-}}, stem of some of the conjugated forms of {{m|fro|finir}}, from {{uder|en|la|fīnīre}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|fīniō}}, from {{m|la|fīnis||end, limit, border, boundary}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰeygʷ-||to stick, set up}} or from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeyd-||to split}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|fĭn'ĭsh}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈfɪnɪʃ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-finnish.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-finish.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-to finish.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪnɪʃ|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|Finnish}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# An [[end]]; the end of anything.
# A [[protective]] [[coating]] given to [[wood]] or [[metal]] and other [[surfaces]].
#: {{ux|en|The car's '''finish''' was so shiny and new.}}
# The result of any process changing the physical or chemical properties of [[cloth]].
# A [[finishing touch]]; careful elaboration; [[polish]].
# {{lb|en|sports}} A [[shot]] on goal, especially one that ends in a goal.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=September 2, 2011
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Bulgaria 0-3 England
|work=BBC
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14770875.stm
|page=
|passage=The Italian opted for Bolton's {{w|Gary Cahill|Cahill}} alongside captain {{w|John Terry}} - and his decision was rewarded with a goal after only 13 minutes. Bulgaria gave a hint of defensive frailties to come when they failed to clear {{w|Ashley Young|Young}}'s corner, and when {{w|Gareth Barry}} found Cahill in the box he applied the '''finish''' past {{w|Nikolay Mihaylov}}.}}

